SU(2) positive-semidefinite boundary-reduction exact audit

COMMAND
make -f Makefile.research probe_su2_psd_boundary_reduction
./probe_su2_psd_boundary_reduction 6 3

OUTPUT
SU2_PSD_BOUNDARY_REDUCTION maximum_level=12 maximum_coefficient=3 profiles=21834 shape_boundary_profiles=318 negative_current_profiles=66 psd_negative_current_profiles=0 diagonal_boundary_profiles=254 diagonal_boundary_failures=2 first_failure=(-1,-1,-1,0) first_profile=[] first_diagonal_failure=(10,4,6,-1) first_diagonal_profile=[1,2,3,3,0,0] result=NO_PSD_BOUNDARY_REDUCTION_FAILURE

METHOD
For every half-level 1<=K<=6, the audit exhausts every nonzero integer
profile with coefficients in {0,1,2,3}.  It retains profiles with
positive vacuum coefficient, log-concavity with interval support, and
every nonnegative simple-current boundary current.  It constructs the
complete finite multiplication matrix

  C_(R,S)=sum_(h=|R-S|)^min(R+S,2K-R-S) d_h

and locates every profile with a negative anchored entry

  d_0 C_(R,S)-d_R d_S.

For each such failure, it tests positive semidefiniteness of C by
computing every principal minor with fraction-free exact integer
elimination.  No target failure has a positive-semidefinite
multiplication matrix.  The zero count is bounded evidence for
positive-semidefinite boundary reduction, not an unbounded proof.

Separately, the audit retains only the diagonal anchored inequalities.
The displayed profile [1,2,3,3,0,0] has all diagonal and boundary
currents nonnegative but the exact off-diagonal current -1.  Hence
diagonal positive semidefiniteness is insufficient.

COMPILER FLAGS
g++ -O2 -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wconversion \
  -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror \
  probe_su2_psd_boundary_reduction.cpp \
  -o probe_su2_psd_boundary_reduction

